1
00:00:01,541 --> 00:00:04,208
I've decided to proceed with radiation
treatment.

2
00:00:04,208 --> 00:00:06,791
What's next?

3
00:00:06,791 --> 00:00:08,416
Once a radiation treatment

4
00:00:08,416 --> 00:00:12,000
plan has been made,
the planning process begins.

5
00:00:12,208 --> 00:00:15,416
This involves gathering any related
imaging studies,

6
00:00:15,416 --> 00:00:19,083
such as previous MRI's or Pet CT scans,

7
00:00:19,083 --> 00:00:22,458
or obtaining an updated one if necessary.

8
00:00:22,708 --> 00:00:26,833
The primary planning step is called a CT
simulation scan.

9
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:30,416
This is where you come into the radiation
oncology department

10
00:00:30,416 --> 00:00:35,666
to get a special CT scan that is used
for radiation treatment purposes.

11
00:00:35,666 --> 00:00:38,166
This scan is different from a regular CT

12
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:40,791
you may have gotten in the past.

13
00:00:40,791 --> 00:00:43,125
For the CT simulation scan,

14
00:00:43,125 --> 00:00:46,083
You will meet the team of radiation
therapists.

15
00:00:46,083 --> 00:00:50,750
They are specially trained professionals
who are experts in acquiring this scan

16
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:54,000
and in operating the radiation treatment
machines.

17
00:00:55,375 --> 00:00:58,750
The therapist will position
you on a CT scanner table

18
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:02,666
in a special way, depending on what
you are getting treatment for.

19
00:01:03,291 --> 00:01:08,583
This may involve creating a special mask
in the shape of your head and face,

20
00:01:09,208 --> 00:01:13,000
or creating bolsters or pillows shaped 
to your body,

21
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,583
or positioning your arms and legs at 
specific angles

22
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:19,791
all depending on your unique situation.

23
00:01:20,458 --> 00:01:23,666
Once you are positioned,
the therapist use stickers

24
00:01:23,666 --> 00:01:27,958
or other indicators
to mark specific locations on your body,

25
00:01:27,958 --> 00:01:31,958
and will take photos
to record the exact method of set up.

26
00:01:32,208 --> 00:01:35,416
This is important
as the position will be replicated

27
00:01:35,416 --> 00:01:38,625
each time you receive radiation treatment.

28
00:01:39,083 --> 00:01:41,291
Then you will undergo a CT scan

29
00:01:41,291 --> 00:01:43,375
in this special position.

30
00:01:45,916 --> 00:01:48,916
Again, depending on your specific plan,

31
00:01:48,916 --> 00:01:51,708
you may receive other special instructions

32
00:01:51,708 --> 00:01:54,833
such as how to breathe
during the scan itself,

33
00:01:54,833 --> 00:01:57,833
or you may need a special dye or contrast

34
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:00,833
to help with illuminating your anatomy.

35
00:02:01,125 --> 00:02:04,791
Once the CT scan is complete,
you will go home.

36
00:02:05,083 --> 00:02:08,416
No radiation
treatment is given on this planning day.

37
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:11,500
This planning CT scan will be used 

38
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,958
in conjunction with your previous images,

39
00:02:13,958 --> 00:02:18,000
if applicable, to determine the radiation
treatment target

40
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,166
and will be used for the radiation
treatment planning.

41
00:02:21,541 --> 00:02:23,875
Planning usually takes up to one week

42
00:02:23,875 --> 00:02:28,000
and includes rigorous checks
with the doctors and medical physics team

43
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,416
for quality assurance to ensure a safe
and accurate delivery of the radiation.
